===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An act or instance of [[rerun#Verb|rerunning]]; a [[repetition]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2017-02-27|author=Mure Dickie; Henry Mance|title=Theresa May’s speech to head off Scottish independence poll|journal=w:Financial Times|url=https://www.ft.com/content/43f094ca-f8f9-11e6-9516-2d969e0d3b65|passage=Even some strong supporters of leaving the UK worry that an early '''re-run''' would be doomed to failure.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2019-07-11|author=John Thornhill|title=Does tech threaten to rerun the worst of the Industrial Revolution?|journal=w:Financial Times|url=https://www.ft.com/content/f1b84f22-a25b-11e9-974c-ad1c6ab5efd1|passage=The central concern that runs through ''The Technology Trap'' is that, unless we are very careful, our latest technological revolution may well turn out to be a tumultuous '''rerun''' of the Industrial Revolution, with dire social and political consequences.}}
# {{lb|en|American}} A [[television program]] shown after its [[initial]] [[presentation]], particularly many weeks after its initial presentation; a [[repeat]].
#: {{syn|en|repeat}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm not a fan of the new ''{{w|The Simpsons|Simpsons}}'' episodes, but the old '''reruns''' have me in stitches, although I've seen them countless times.}}
# {{lb|en|publishing}} Another printing run (impression; batch of copies of a given edition) of a [[book]], [[cartoon]], etc.
# {{lb|en|political}} A [[political]] [[candidate]] who holds the same political [[agenda]] or [[doctrine]] as a past or incumbent holder of a given political office.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A second or subsequent run of a [[computer program]].
#: {{ux|en|Each '''rerun''' of the algorithm takes several minutes.}}

